mechanism:
Inspect the current scene tree, schedule frontier, navigation geometry, and persistent no-go zone to identify the two unfinished goals whose navigation reachability is broken, the transport-team gathering location that cannot be reached, and any planned descendant poses whose placement depends on the moved geometry.

Edit only the scene tree so that:
- each identified unfinished goal has a legal destination reachable by the required navigating transport agent without entering the no-go zone;
- the affected transport team has a legally reachable gathering pose;
- staging, deposit, and lift poses associated with relocated work remain mutually consistent;
- required relative transforms within each affected sub-assembly are preserved; and
- every remaining navigation-dependent node has a reachable required pose, allowing the previously frozen downstream nodes eventually to close.

The implementation may relocate the smallest suitable affected scene-tree subtree, relocate staging poses, translate the larger build placement, or combine these methods. The particular displacement and subtree choice are open, provided the stated reachability and transform-preservation effects hold. The tool discovers all identities, poses, offsets, and distances from the world when it runs.

The no-go zone remains unchanged. The schedule DAG and assignment edges are not directly edited, and the tool does not formulate, commit, or re-solve the MILP. It consumes intervention time and the ordinary energy required to enact any necessary physical repositioning, but consumes no body replacement or other scarce spare. Preconditions are that a legal alternative placement exists and that already fixed geometry does not make the required scene-tree relocation physically impossible. The scene-tree edits are reversible by restoring the prior transforms before incompatible downstream placement occurs; physical repositioning already performed still costs time and energy and may not be freely reversible after later assembly work closes.
